        {
          "subsection_id": "pop_bandage_subsection_2",
          "subsection_title": "1. Introduction and definition",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "pop_bandage_group_2",
              "group_title": "",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "A Plaster of Paris (POP) bandage is a roll of gauze impregnated with calcium sulfate hemihydrate powder. When immersed in water, the plaster becomes workable and can be applied around a limb or used to construct a plaster slab. It subsequently hardens through a chemical setting reaction to provide rigid immobilisation."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "It is commonly used in orthopaedics and emergency departments for fracture immobilisation, maintaining reduction and supporting injured limbs."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Key identifying feature: A gauze bandage coated with white plaster powder that becomes pliable when wetted and rigid after setting."
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "group_id": "pop_bandage_group_3",
              "group_title": "Distinguish these three terms",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "table",
                  "columns": [
                    "Term",
                    "Meaning"
                  ],
                  "rows": [
                    [
                      "POP material",
                      "Calcium sulfate hemihydrate powder"
                    ],
                    [
                      "POP bandage",
                      "Gauze roll impregnated with the plaster powder"
                    ],
                    [
                      "POP cast",
                      "The rigid immobilisation formed after the material is applied and sets"
                    ]
                  ]
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "subsection_id": "pop_bandage_subsection_3",
          "subsection_title": "2. Identification in an OSCE station",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "pop_bandage_group_4",
              "group_title": "",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "image",
                  "assetPath": "assets/resources/content/surgical/pop_bandage_04.webp",
                  "caption": null
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Model identification"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "“This is a Plaster of Paris bandage, an orthopaedic casting material consisting of gauze impregnated with calcium sulfate hemihydrate. It is wetted, applied and moulded to the required anatomical shape, where it sets to form a rigid supporting structure.”",
                  "style": "quote"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Key points"
                },
                {
                  "type": "list",
                  "items": [
                    {
                      "text": "Supplied in rolls.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Requires water for activation.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Can be layered to achieve the required strength.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Used to construct complete casts and plaster slabs.",
                      "level": 0
                    }
                  ]
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "subsection_id": "pop_bandage_subsection_4",
          "subsection_title": "3. Parts and labelling",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "pop_bandage_group_5",
              "group_title": "",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "A POP bandage is a relatively simple device. In an OSCE, identify the following features where visible."
                },
                {
                  "type": "image",
                  "assetPath": "assets/resources/content/surgical/pop_bandage_05.webp",
                  "caption": null
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "1. Bandage roll"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "The cylindrical roll of gauze that can be unwound during application."
                },
                {
                  "type": "image",
                  "assetPath": "assets/resources/content/surgical/pop_bandage_06.webp",
                  "caption": null
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "2. Plaster-impregnated gauze"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "The woven substrate coated with the plaster compound. It provides the framework that holds the material together."
                },
                {
                  "type": "image",
                  "assetPath": "assets/resources/content/surgical/pop_bandage_07.webp",
                  "caption": null
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "3. Packaging and label"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "May indicate roll width, length, manufacturer, batch number, expiry date and storage instructions."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Unlike a Foley catheter, a POP bandage does not have multiple functional ports or mechanical parts. Its important identifying features are the roll, plaster coating and product label."
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "subsection_id": "pop_bandage_subsection_5",
          "subsection_title": "4. Classification and types",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "pop_bandage_group_6",
              "group_title": "A. According to setting properties",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "list",
                  "items": [
                    {
                      "text": "Conventional POP bandage: Sets through hydration of the plaster compound and is widely used for casting.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Fast-setting POP bandage: Formulated to set more rapidly, depending on the product.",
                      "level": 0
                    }
                  ]
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "group_id": "pop_bandage_group_7",
              "group_title": "B. According to dimensions",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "POP bandages are available in different widths and lengths. Common widths include approximately 5 cm, 7.5 cm, 10 cm, 15 cm and 20 cm, although availability varies by manufacturer."
                },
                {
                  "type": "list",
                  "items": [
                    {
                      "text": "Narrow rolls are useful for smaller areas and detailed applications.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Medium-width rolls are useful for forearms and similar regions.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Wider rolls can cover larger limbs more efficiently.",
                      "level": 0
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "The appropriate width depends on the patient's anatomy and the cast or slab being constructed."
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "group_id": "pop_bandage_group_8",
              "group_title": "C. According to application",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "list",
                  "items": [
                    {
                      "text": "Circumferential casting: Multiple layers are applied around the limb.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Plaster slab or backslab: Several layers are arranged as a flat or curved slab and secured with a separate bandage.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Specialist moulded casts: Used for selected paediatric, orthopaedic and deformity-correction applications.",
                      "level": 0
                    }
                  ]
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "subsection_id": "pop_bandage_subsection_6",
          "subsection_title": "5. Composition and mechanism of action",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "pop_bandage_group_9",
              "group_title": "",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "POP bandages contain calcium sulfate hemihydrate on a gauze substrate."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "When wetted, the compound reacts with water and re-forms calcium sulfate dihydrate crystals. The interlocking crystals produce a rigid structure and release heat."
                },
                {
                  "type": "image",
                  "assetPath": "assets/resources/content/surgical/pop_bandage_08.webp",
                  "caption": null
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Viva point: Setting is an exothermic chemical reaction, not simply the evaporation of water."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "The material must therefore be handled correctly to avoid thermal injury, especially when thick layers are applied."
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "subsection_id": "pop_bandage_subsection_7",
          "subsection_title": "6. Indications",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "pop_bandage_group_10",
              "group_title": "",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "list",
                  "title": "POP bandages are used for:",
                  "items": [
                    {
                      "text": "Immobilisation of selected fractures.",
                      "level": 0,
                      "label": "1."
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Maintaining a fracture reduction.",
                      "level": 0,
                      "label": "2."
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Constructing a backslab during the acute phase of an injury.",
                      "level": 0,
                      "label": "3."
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Supporting selected ligament and soft-tissue injuries.",
                      "level": 0,
                      "label": "4."
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Immobilisation after selected orthopaedic procedures.",
                      "level": 0,
                      "label": "5."
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Selected paediatric fracture management.",
                      "level": 0,
                      "label": "6."
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Serial casting for selected deformities, including clubfoot treatment.",
                      "level": 0,
                      "label": "7."
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Constructing specialised orthopaedic casts when prescribed.",
                      "level": 0,
                      "label": "8."
                    }
                  ]
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "group_id": "pop_bandage_group_11",
              "group_title": "Examples of fractures",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "list",
                  "items": [
                    {
                      "text": "Distal radius fractures.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Selected forearm fractures.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Selected metacarpal fractures.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Selected paediatric fractures.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Selected ankle and foot fractures.",
                      "level": 0
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Not all fractures can be managed with POP alone. Unstable, open or significantly displaced fractures and injuries with neurovascular compromise require appropriate orthopaedic assessment."
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "subsection_id": "pop_bandage_subsection_8",
          "subsection_title": "7. Equipment required",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "pop_bandage_group_12",
              "group_title": "",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "image",
                  "assetPath": "assets/resources/content/surgical/pop_bandage_09.webp",
                  "caption": null
                },
                {
                  "type": "image",
                  "assetPath": "assets/resources/content/surgical/pop_bandage_10.webp",
                  "caption": null
                },
                {
                  "type": "image",
                  "assetPath": "assets/resources/content/surgical/pop_bandage_11.webp",
                  "caption": null
                },
                {
                  "type": "list",
                  "title": "Common equipment includes:",
                  "items": [
                    {
                      "text": "POP bandage rolls.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Bowl of water at an appropriate temperature.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Stockinette, where indicated.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Orthopaedic padding.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Gloves and an apron.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Bandage scissors.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "A support for positioning the limb.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Appropriate equipment for distal neurovascular assessment.",
                      "level": 0
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "The exact requirements depend on whether a slab, complete cast or specialised cast is being applied."
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "subsection_id": "pop_bandage_subsection_9",
          "subsection_title": "8. Principles of application",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "pop_bandage_group_13",
              "group_title": "",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "OSCE practical sequence"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Step 1 — Assess the patient"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Confirm the indication, inspect the injury and document baseline distal circulation, sensation and movement. Arrange appropriate imaging and fracture reduction when required."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Step 2 — Prepare and pad"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Position the limb correctly and apply stockinette and adequate padding. Protect bony prominences and avoid wrinkles."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Step 3 — Immerse the bandage"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Dip the roll in appropriately tempered water according to the manufacturer's instructions. Allow the bandage to become thoroughly wetted and observe the recommended soaking procedure."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Step 4 — Apply the bandage"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Unroll smoothly, maintaining even overlap and the prescribed number of layers. Avoid unnecessary tension, bunching or creases."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Step 5 — Mould the plaster"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Mould with the palms rather than fingertips to prevent focal indentations and pressure points. Maintain the required position while the material sets."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Step 6 — Check and monitor"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Ensure the cast or slab is appropriately shaped, the edges are safe and the limb is not constricted. Reassess distal neurovascular status and document the findings."
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "group_id": "pop_bandage_group_14",
              "group_title": "Technical principles the examiner may ask about",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Why is padding necessary?"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "It protects the skin and bony prominences from pressure, friction and thermal injury."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Why is the bandage overlapped?"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "To create a continuous, adequately reinforced structure without weak gaps."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Why should excessive tension be avoided?"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Excessive tension can constrict the limb as swelling develops."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Why are the palms used for moulding?"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "The fingertips can create local depressions that become pressure points after the plaster hardens."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Why must the limb be supported during setting?"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "The material is vulnerable to deformation until it has adequately hardened."
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "subsection_id": "pop_bandage_subsection_10",
          "subsection_title": "9. Advantages and disadvantages",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "pop_bandage_group_15",
              "group_title": "Advantages",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "list",
                  "items": [
                    {
                      "text": "Inexpensive.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Widely available.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Excellent mouldability.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Conforms well to irregular contours.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Can be used for slabs and complete casts.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Provides effective immobilisation in appropriate fractures.",
                      "level": 0
                    }
                  ]
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "group_id": "pop_bandage_group_16",
              "group_title": "Disadvantages",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "list",
                  "items": [
                    {
                      "text": "Requires water and preparation.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Sets more slowly than many synthetic casting materials.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Relatively heavy.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Weakens when wet.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Setting reaction produces heat.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Can cause pressure injury if incorrectly applied.",
                      "level": 0
                    }
                  ]
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "subsection_id": "pop_bandage_subsection_11",
          "subsection_title": "10. Complications",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "pop_bandage_group_17",
              "group_title": "",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "table",
                  "columns": [
                    "Complication",
                    "Cause or significance"
                  ],
                  "rows": [
                    [
                      "Thermal burns",
                      "Excessive heat during setting, particularly with thick applications or hot water"
                    ],
                    [
                      "Pressure sores",
                      "Poor padding, wrinkles or focal pressure"
                    ],
                    [
                      "Neurovascular compromise",
                      "Excessive constriction or swelling within a cast"
                    ],
                    [
                      "Compartment syndrome",
                      "Increased pressure within a closed fascial compartment"
                    ],
                    [
                      "Skin maceration",
                      "Moisture trapped beneath the cast"
                    ],
                    [
                      "Loss of reduction",
                      "Inadequate moulding, poor fit or changing swelling"
                    ],
                    [
                      "Cast breakdown",
                      "Damage or excessive moisture"
                    ],
                    [
                      "Joint stiffness",
                      "Prolonged immobilisation"
                    ],
                    [
                      "Muscle wasting",
                      "Reduced use of the limb"
                    ]
                  ]
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "group_id": "pop_bandage_group_18",
              "group_title": "Emergency signs to recognise",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Increasing pain, pain on passive stretch, new paraesthesia, progressive swelling, weakness or pale/cold digits require urgent assessment. A palpable pulse does not exclude compartment syndrome."
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "subsection_id": "pop_bandage_subsection_12",
          "subsection_title": "11. POP bandage versus ordinary crepe bandage",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "pop_bandage_group_19",
              "group_title": "",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "table",
                  "columns": [
                    "Feature",
                    "POP bandage",
                    "Crepe bandage"
                  ],
                  "rows": [
                    [
                      "Material",
                      "Plaster-impregnated gauze",
                      "Elasticated or woven stretch fabric"
                    ],
                    [
                      "Setting",
                      "Hardens after wetting",
                      "Does not chemically harden"
                    ],
                    [
                      "Main purpose",
                      "Rigid immobilisation",
                      "Compression, support and securing dressings"
                    ],
                    [
                      "Mouldability",
                      "Moulds and hardens to the limb",
                      "Conforms but remains flexible"
                    ],
                    [
                      "Swelling accommodation",
                      "Limited in a rigid circumferential cast",
                      "More flexible, though excessive compression is still hazardous"
                    ],
                    [
                      "Removal",
                      "Often requires a cast saw or appropriate cutting technique",
                      "Usually unwound"
                    ],
                    [
                      "Water sensitivity",
                      "Conventional POP deteriorates when wet",
                      "Depends on the bandage material and product"
                    ]
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Exam trap: A crepe bandage cannot replace a properly applied rigid cast when rigid fracture immobilisation is required."
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
