        {
          "subsection_id": "pop_bandage_subsection_14",
          "subsection_title": "13. Model OSCE presentation",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "pop_bandage_group_21",
              "group_title": "",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "“This is a Plaster of Paris bandage, an orthopaedic casting material consisting of gauze impregnated with calcium sulfate hemihydrate. It is wetted and applied in layers, then moulded to the required anatomical position, where it hardens through an exothermic chemical reaction. It is used for immobilising selected fractures, maintaining reduction and constructing plaster slabs and casts. Its advantages include low cost, availability and excellent mouldability. Its disadvantages include relatively heavy weight, slower setting and poor water resistance. Complications include thermal burns, pressure sores, neurovascular compromise and compartment syndrome. Safe application requires adequate padding, even overlap, appropriate moulding, neurovascular assessment and patient education.”",
